1. Whenever something happens on a Linux server, it is usually recorded in a log file.

As a Linux Administrator, if a user reports:

"I can't login."
"SSH is not working."
"Disk is full."
"Server restarted automatically."
"Application crashed."

The first place you should check is the logs.

2. What is a Log File?

A log file is a file that stores information about events occurring on the Linux system.

These events include:

User login/logout
System startup and shutdown
Errors
Warnings
Service status
Security events
Application activity

3. Real-Life Example

Think of a CCTV camera.

If something is stolen in an office, security reviews the CCTV footage.

Similarly,

Linux logs act like a CCTV recording for your server.

They record what happened, when it happened, and sometimes who performed the action.

4. Where are Linux Logs Stored?

Most Linux log files are stored in:

/var/log/

5. Log Files

| Log File                                              | Purpose                     | When to Check                     |
| ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| `/var/log/syslog` *(Ubuntu/Debian)*                   | General system messages     | System issues, services           |
| `/var/log/messages` *(RHEL/CentOS)*                   | General system messages     | System troubleshooting            |
| `/var/log/auth.log` *(Ubuntu/Debian)*                 | Authentication and SSH logs | Login failures, sudo, SSH         |
| `/var/log/secure` *(RHEL/CentOS)*                     | Authentication and SSH logs | Login and security events         |
| `/var/log/kern.log`                                   | Kernel messages             | Driver and hardware issues        |
| `/var/log/dmesg`                                      | Boot-time kernel messages   | Boot and hardware troubleshooting |
| `/var/log/boot.log`                                   | Boot process logs           | Startup problems                  |
| `/var/log/faillog`                                    | Failed login attempts       | Security auditing                 |
| `/var/log/lastlog`                                    | Last login details          | User login history                |
| `/var/log/wtmp`                                       | Login/logout history        | Audit user sessions               |
| `/var/log/btmp`                                       | Failed login history        | Detect brute-force attacks        |
| `/var/log/nginx/access.log`                           | Nginx access requests       | Website traffic                   |
| `/var/log/nginx/error.log`                            | Nginx errors                | Website troubleshooting           |
| `/var/log/apache2/access.log` *(Ubuntu)*              | Apache access requests      | Website traffic                   |
| `/var/log/apache2/error.log` *(Ubuntu)*               | Apache errors               | Website issues                    |
| `/var/log/mysql/error.log` *(path varies)*            | MySQL server errors         | Database troubleshooting          |
| `/var/log/cron` *(RHEL)*                              | Cron job execution          | Scheduled task troubleshooting    |
| `/var/log/mail.log` *(Ubuntu)*                        | Mail server activity        | Email issues                      |
| `/var/log/audit/audit.log` *(if auditd is installed)* | Security audit events       | Compliance and auditing           |

6. Let's understand in details

a)Failed Login History
/var/log/btmp

Read using

sudo lastb

Useful for identifying repeated failed login attempts.

b)Cron Logs

RHEL

/var/log/cron

Ubuntu typically records cron activity in:

/var/log/syslog

Example

grep CRON /var/log/syslog
c)Mail Logs

Ubuntu

/var/log/mail.log

Contains mail server activity.

d)Audit Logs

If auditd is installed:

/var/log/audit/audit.log

Contains:

File access
Security policy events
User actions

Useful for compliance and security investigations.

e)Last Login Information
/var/log/lastlog

Command

lastlog

Example

Username         Latest
bilal            Tue Jul 29

f)Failed Login Attempts
/var/log/faillog

View

faillog

Shows users with failed login attempts.

g)dmesg Log
/var/log/dmesg

Contains kernel boot messages.

Better command:

dmesg

Example

dmesg | less

Check USB

dmesg | grep USB

Check disk

dmesg | grep sda

g)Boot Logs
/var/log/boot.log

Contains messages generated during system startup.

Useful if the system fails to boot correctly.

7. journalctl (Modern Linux)

Many modern Linux distributions use systemd-journald.

Instead of reading individual files,

use

journalctl

Show all logs

journalctl

Recent logs

journalctl -n 50

Live logs

journalctl -f

Logs since today

journalctl --since today

Logs for SSH

journalctl -u ssh

Ubuntu service name may be ssh; RHEL is commonly sshd.

Useful Commands for Monitoring Logs
View entire file
cat /var/log/syslog
